Options:

Computation of the binomial ideal associated to B via the function *note
binomialIdeal: binomialIdeal, can be done via two different methods:

The standard method uses the Macaulay2 function *note kernel:
(Macaulay2Doc)kernel, and requires no configuration.

Alternatively, the external program 4ti2, http://www.4ti2.de/ can be called,
which is delivered with the Macaulay2 distribution. For large examples this is
typically much faster. To use this option load the MonomialAlgebras package
with the following configuration option:

*note loadPackage: (Macaulay2Doc)loadPackage,("MonomialAlgebras",Configuration
=>\{"Use4ti2"=>true\})

Note that you can change the standard option by editing the file
init-MonomialAlgebras.m2 in the .Macaulay directory in your home directory.

In order to use 4ti2, on some systems you may have to configure the Macaulay2
package FourTiTwo first.



Setup:

Install this *note Package: (Macaulay2Doc)Package, by doing

*note installPackage: (Macaulay2Doc)installPackage,("MonomialAlgebras")

Tests:

The following files contain the tuples (regularity, degree, codim) for every
semigroup B in \mathbb{N}^d with fixed d and fixed coordinate sum a. Using this
data we have verified the Eisenbud-Goto conjecture in these cases.

d = 3:

a = 3:    proveEG_33 (see
https://agag-jboehm.math.rptu.de/~boehm/Macaulay2/MonomialAlgebras/proveEG_33.
m2 )

a = 4:    proveEG_43 (see
https://agag-jboehm.math.rptu.de/~boehm/Macaulay2/MonomialAlgebras/proveEG_43.
m2 )

a = 5:    proveEG_53 (see
https://agag-jboehm.math.rptu.de/~boehm/Macaulay2/MonomialAlgebras/proveEG_53.
zip )

d = 4:

a = 2:    proveEG_24 (see
https://agag-jboehm.math.rptu.de/~boehm/Macaulay2/MonomialAlgebras/proveEG_24.
m2 )

a = 3:    proveEG_34 (see
https://agag-jboehm.math.rptu.de/~boehm/Macaulay2/MonomialAlgebras/proveEG_34.
zip )

d = 5:

a = 2:    proveEG_25 (see
https://agag-jboehm.math.rptu.de/~boehm/Macaulay2/MonomialAlgebras/proveEG_25.
zip )



